.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt HEAD 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm head
.Nd print the beginning of files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar lines | Fl c Ar bytes
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Print the first ten lines of each file by default.
Stop once the requested count is available, using buffered reads.
.Pp
.Fl n Ar count
selects lines;
.Fl c Ar count
selects bytes.
Counts are nonnegative decimal integers; zero selects no data.
Repeated count options are accepted with the last one taking effect.
Options must precede paths; there is no
.Ql --
terminator and no attached-count syntax such as
.Ql -n3 .
Use
.Ql ./-name
for a dash-leading file name.
.Pp
With no files, read standard input.
An explicit
.Ql -
is a file name, not standard input.
With multiple files, print a
.Ql ==> file <==
header for each file processed, separated by blank lines.
Files must be readable through this process's view; nothing is modified.
Input errors on named files are diagnosed on standard error and processing
continues.
An error while processing standard input sets status 1 without a diagnostic.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 for successful processing; 1 for an observed read or copy failure;
2 for invalid options or counts.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
head -n 3 /var/log/sshd
cat /etc/network.conf | head -c 20
.Ed
.Sh CAVEATS
Final output-flush failures are not reflected reliably in this command's status.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cat 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
